"summary":{
 "sign_reliability":"High. I found no wrong signs (0 wrong_sign out of ~110 labels). The only uncertain sign is 231 in 2228552, where the effect itself is two-sided.",
 "typical_errors":[
  "Generic, overlapping directions padded into secondary as near-duplicates: 1 (applied to in-kind services, health coverage and local tax), 20/19 (duplicating 12/15 in health bills), 41, 312, 197, 17 (duplicating 211). These are usually tertiary or not applicable.",
  "Missed value-laden or ideological directions, even when they are the core of the bill: 151− (Shabbat trains), 178+ (benefits revoked for terrorism), 44+ (Knesset approval of civil procedure rules), 202+ (leave for single fathers), 90− (new ground for arrest), 167− (Israeli law in Judea and Samaria).",
  "Missed specialized directions that are close to the core: 271 (drugs), 253 (special education), 247 (safe rooms), 25 (switching health funds), 206 (disabilities).",
  "The real primary is sometimes placed in secondary rather than main (178, 143, 169, 99, 44). main_verdict was still 'correct' in 24 of 25 bills, and 'partly' only for 2225690.",
  "Directions used outside their definitions: 64 for creating a new offense or an arrest ground, 97 for missile protection, 365 for state data checks, 227 for access to credit."],
 "bias":"The classifier systematically leans toward economic, welfare and consumer directions (1, 11, 19, 20, 35, 17), which it adds by default. It under-labels the political and ideological directions (religion and state, security and terrorism, Knesset versus judiciary, suspects' rights), which are the ones that matter most for positioning MKs. Signs show no bias, and main labels are almost always reasonable. The error is in coverage, and it skews the profile toward socio-economic directions."}
}
